  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "For how many positive integers \\(m\\) does there exist at least one positive integer \\(n\\) such that \\(m\\cdot n\\le m+n?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(6\\)",
    "C": "\\(9\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\)",
    "E": "infinitely many"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "We need only one suitable positive integer \\(n\\) for each \\(m\\). Choose \\(n=1\\). Then, for every positive integer \\(m\\),\n\\[\nmn=m\\le m+1=m+n.\n\\]\nThus every positive integer \\(m\\) qualifies. Since there are infinitely many positive integers, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{infinitely many}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
